# aeneid-002 draft: each unit names where it ENDS (line, closing text); t is cut from the source by aeneid_lib.cut.
U = [
((103,"tollit."),
"To him hurling such words, a gale shrieking from the North\nstrikes the sail full in front, and lifts the waves to the stars.",
"Even as he cries out so, a squall screaming down from the north strikes full against the sail and heaves the waves up to the stars.",
"'iactanti' — dative, 'to him (Aeneas) flinging out such words', the person affected by what happens; 'talia' picks up his outcry of lines 94–101, so the storm answers him as he speaks. 'Aquilone' — the North Wind, ablative ('with, from'). 'adversa' agrees with 'procella': the gust comes from dead ahead. 'ad sidera' — to the stars, epic exaggeration. The whole storm is rebuilt from Odysseus' storm in Odyssey 5, where a great wave falls on him just as he finishes wishing he had died at Troy — the order Virgil keeps here."),
((104,"remi;"),
"The oars are shattered;",
"The oars snap;",
"Verb first, clause short: from here to line 107 the damage comes in a run of quick blows, a clause for each. 'Franguntur' — 'are broken', present tense, the vivid narrative present that carries most of the storm."),
((105,"latus;"),
"then the prow swings away, and to the waves\ngives its side;",
"then the prow swings round and lays the ship's side open to the waves;",
"'avertit' is used without an object here, 'turns away'. With the bow knocked off course the ship lies broadside to the seas, the most dangerous position a ship can be in. 'undis' — dative, 'to the waves'."),
((105,"mons."),
"there follows in a heap a sheer mountain of water.",
"and after it comes a sheer mountain of water, piled high.",
"'cumulo' — 'in a heap', ablative of manner. The line ends on a single-syllable word, 'mons', which Virgil rarely allows at the close of a hexameter: the rhythm breaks as the wave breaks. 'aquae mons' — 'a mountain of water', a picture from the Odyssey's storms."),
((106,"pendent;"),
"These on the topmost wave hang;",
"Some hang on the crest of a wave;",
"'Hi… his' — 'these… to those': the crews of different ships, some lifted high and some dropped low. 'summo in fluctu' — 'on the top of the wave': 'summus' often means 'the top part of' something rather than 'highest'."),
((107,"aperit;"),
"to those the wave, gaping,\nthe earth between the waves lays open;",
"for others the water gapes and bares the sea-floor between the waves;",
"'his' — dative, 'for these'. 'dehiscens' — splitting open. 'terram' is the sea-bed itself, glimpsed at the bottom of the trough. 'inter fluctus' — between the walls of water on either side."),
((107,"harenis."),
"the surge rages with sand.",
"the surge seethes with sand.",
"'harenis' — 'with sands', ablative: the churned water is thick with sand torn from the bottom. Greenough spells 'harena' with h, the older form; 'arena' is also found. The plural of a mass noun ('sands') is common in verse."),
((110,"summo;"),
"Three the South Wind, snatched away, onto hidden rocks hurls —\nrocks which the Italians, out in mid-waves, call the Altars —\na huge ridge at the sea's surface;",
"Three ships the South Wind seizes and flings on hidden rocks — the Italians call them the Altars, those rocks out in mid-sea — a monstrous reef along the surface of the water;",
"'Tris' = 'tres' (with 'navis' understood), Greenough's -is accusative plural, the old ending of i-stem words (you'll see this constantly: 'navis', 'omnis', 'Syrtis'). 'Notus' is the South Wind; 'Eurus', in the next line, the East. Between the dashes is a learned aside, with the relative 'quae' placed late and its antecedent 'saxa' pulled to the front: 'rocks which the Italians call Altars'. Virgil speaks here as a Roman of his own day. The Altars were reefs between Sicily and Africa; which reefs exactly was already disputed in antiquity. 'dorsum' — a back, a ridge: the reef shows like the back of a sea-beast. 'mari summo' — at the top of the sea, just at the surface."),
((112,"harenae."),
"three the East Wind from the deep\ninto the shallows and the Syrtes drives, pitiful to see,\nand dashes them on the shoals, and with a rampart encircles of sand.",
"three more the East Wind drives off the open sea into the shallows and the Syrtes — a pitiful sight — dashes them on the shoals, and walls them round with banks of sand.",
"'ab alto' — 'from the deep', the open sea ('altum' as a noun, which you'll meet often). 'brevia' — 'short places', shallows, a neuter adjective used as a noun. The Syrtes are two great gulfs of shifting sandbanks off the coast of Libya, notorious to ancient sailors; 'Syrtis' is again the -is accusative plural. 'urguet' is Greenough's spelling of 'urget' (The Latin Library prints 'urget': the same word). 'miserabile visu' — 'pitiful to see': 'visu' is the supine in -u, 'in the seeing', used with an adjective. 'aggere harenae' — 'with a rampart of sand': the stranded ships are as good as besieged."),
((115,"ferit:"),
"One, which carried the Lycians and loyal Orontes,\nbefore the man's own eyes a huge sea from on high\nstrikes upon the stern:",
"One ship, which carried the Lycians and loyal Orontes, takes a monstrous sea from overhead full on the stern, before Aeneas's own eyes:",
"'Unam' (navem) stands first as the object, and its subject, 'pontus', does not arrive until the end of the next line: the reader waits, as the ship does, for the blow. 'ipsius' — 'of the man himself', Aeneas, who watches helpless. The Lycians, from south-west Asia Minor, fought for Troy in the Iliad under Sarpedon. 'Oronten' is a Greek accusative (Orontes); Greek names in Virgil often keep their Greek endings. 'a vertice' — 'from the top', from overhead. 'pontus' — the sea (a Greek word), here a single towering wave."),
((116,"caput;"),
"is shaken out, and headlong the helmsman\nis rolled onto his head;",
"the helmsman is flung out and pitched headfirst into the sea;",
"'excutitur' — 'is shaken out', thrown from the ship. 'magister' here is the helmsman, the master of the ship, not a teacher. 'volvitur in caput' — 'is rolled onto his head', tumbling headfirst. A helmsman lost overboard recalls Odyssey 12, where Odysseus' helmsman is struck down when Zeus wrecks the ship; Aeneas will lose another, Palinurus, at the end of Book 5."),
((117,"vortex."),
"but her three times the wave, in that same spot,\nwhirls, driving her round, and the racing swallows her in the sea whirlpool.",
"while the ship herself is spun three times round on the spot by the driving water, and a racing whirlpool gulps her down into the sea.",
"'ast' is an old form of 'at', 'but', kept in epic for its archaic ring. 'illam' — the ship. 'ter' — 'three times', the usual number of epic repetition. 'torquet agens circum' — 'twists her, driving her round'. 'vortex' is Greenough's spelling of 'vertex' (whirlpool), the older vowel, like 'volgus' and 'divom'. 'aequore' — ablative of place, 'in the sea'. The line ends on the whirlpool, as the ship does."),
((119,"undas."),
"There appear, scattered, men swimming in the vast abyss,\narms of men, and planks, and Trojan treasure across the waves.",
"Here and there swimmers show in the vast flood, and over the waves drift the arms of men, timbers, and the treasure of Troy.",
"'Adparent' is Greenough's spelling of 'apparent': he keeps the prefix unassimilated (ad-, not ap-). 'rari' — few and far between. 'gurgite' — properly a whirlpool or abyss, here the open sea. 'arma virum' — 'arms of men', with 'virum' the old genitive plural again — and an echo of the poem's first words, 'arma virumque': war and the man are going down together. 'gaza' is a Persian word for royal treasure, borrowed into Greek and Latin: a touch of eastern wealth for Troy."),
((122,"hiems;"),
"Now the stout ship of Ilioneus, now that of brave Achates,\nand that in which Abas rode, and that in which aged Aletes,\nthe storm has overcome;",
"Now the sturdy ship of Ilioneus, now brave Achates', now the one that carried Abas and the one that bore old Aletes — the storm has beaten them all;",
"The roll-call of captains, held over two lines before the verb, tolls the losses one by one: 'iam… iam' ('now… now'), 'et qua… et qua'. 'Ilionei' is the genitive of Ilioneus, the -ei run together into one syllable (synizesis); 'Achati' is the genitive of Achates, a Greek name in -es declined as Latin. 'qua (navi) vectus (est)' — 'in which Abas was carried'; the verb is left out, and left out again with Aletes. 'hiems' — 'winter', regularly 'storm' in verse. 'grandaevus' — of great age."),
((123,"fatiscunt."),
"with loosened joints of their sides, all\ntake in the hostile water, and with cracks they gape.",
"with the seams of their hulls sprung, all of them take in the enemy water and split open at every crack.",
"'laxis laterum compagibus' — ablative absolute, 'the joinings of their sides having been loosened'. 'imbrem' — properly rain; here seawater, pouring in like a downpour. 'inimicum' — hostile: the water boards the ships like an enemy. 'rimis' — 'with cracks', ablative of means; 'fatiscunt' — gape, come apart. Read 'inimicum imbrem' with elision, 'inimic' imbrem'."),
((126,"commotus;"),
"Meanwhile that with great roaring the sea was in turmoil,\nand that the storm was let loose, Neptune perceived, and that from the lowest\ndepths the still waters were poured back up — gravely stirred;",
"Meanwhile Neptune sensed the great roar of the sea in turmoil, the storm let loose, and the still waters churned up from the depths, and he was deeply troubled;",
"A long accusative and infinitive, all hanging on 'sensit': 'pontum misceri' (that the sea was being thrown into confusion), 'hiemem emissam (esse)' (that the storm had been let loose), 'stagna refusa (esse)' (that the still waters had been poured back). 'magno… murmure' — the m's rumble through the line. 'stagna' are the motionless waters of the sea-bed, the god's own domain, stirred now from 'imis vadis', the lowest depths. Neptune is Homer's Poseidon, but turned round: in Odyssey 5 Poseidon raises the storm against the hero; here the sea-god ends a storm he did not send. 'graviter commotus' — deeply stirred, angry."),
((127,"unda."),
"and over the deep\nlooking out, from the top of the wave his calm head he raised.",
"and gazing out over the deep, he lifted his calm head above the crest of the waves.",
"'alto' — over the deep, the open sea. 'placidum' sits in pointed tension with 'graviter commotus' just before: angry within, the god shows the sea a calm face, and his calm is already the calm he will give the water. Readers have argued over the pairing since antiquity; the point is a ruler's self-command. 'summa… unda' — 'from the top of the wave', ablative of the place from which."),
((130,"irae."),
"Scattered, Aeneas's fleet over the whole sea he sees,\nby the waves overwhelmed the Trojans, and by the sky's collapse;\nnor were hidden from her brother the tricks of Juno and her angers.",
"He sees Aeneas's fleet scattered over the whole sea, the Trojans crushed by the waves and the falling sky; nor did Juno's tricks and rage escape her brother.",
"'Disiectam… classem' frames the line, the broken fleet spread across it (Greenough prints a comma after 'Aeneae'). 'Troas' is a Greek accusative plural (Tros, 'a Trojan'), with short -as. 'caeli ruina' — 'the collapse of the sky', the cloudburst, as if heaven were falling in. 'latuere' = 'latuerunt' (the -ere ending again); 'latere' with an accusative of the person means 'to escape someone's notice', and 'fratrem' is the one not deceived. Neptune and Juno are both children of Saturn: he knows his sister's ways. 'irae' — plural again, her resentments, as in line 11."),
((131,"fatur:"),
"Eurus to him and Zephyrus he calls, then such words speaks:",
"He summons the East Wind and the West, and then addresses them in these words:",
"Eurus (east) and Zephyrus (west) stand for all the winds. 'dehinc' — 'then', scanned as one syllable (synizesis). 'talia fatur' — 'speaks such things', a formula that introduces a speech, here running from the next line."),
((132,"vestri?"),
"“Has such great confidence in your birth taken hold of you?",
"“Has pride in your ancestry so taken hold of you?",
"Neptune opens with indignant questions; '-ne' on 'Tanta' marks the question. 'generis vestri' — 'in your race': in Hesiod's Theogony the winds are sons of the Titan Astraeus and the Dawn, a Titan brood with Titan arrogance. The speech is short and sharp, a magistrate's rebuke rather than a god's tirade."),
((134,"moles?"),
"Now heaven and earth without my divine assent, winds,\nto throw into confusion, and such masses do you dare to raise?",
"Do you now dare, you winds, to throw heaven and earth into turmoil without my sanction, and pile up such mountains of water?",
"'Iam' — 'now actually', indignant: you have come to this. 'meo sine numine' — the preposition between adjective and noun, a common verse order; 'numen' here is divine will and authority — without Neptune's consent, and in his own realm. Both infinitives hang on 'audetis'. 'moles' — masses, the mountainous waves. 'caelum terramque miscere' is exactly what Aeolus's restraint was there to prevent (lines 58–59)."),
((135,"ego—"),
"Whom I —",
"You whom I —",
"The most famous break-off in Latin poetry, the figure rhetoricians call aposiopesis ('falling silent'): the threat — 'quos ego… (you) whom I (will punish)…' — is left unspoken, and the unsaid verb weighs more than any said one. Homer's angry gods threaten in full; Virgil's ruler masters himself in mid-sentence, and that self-mastery is the point of the scene. 'Quos ego' became proverbial for a threat held back. 'quos' is a connecting relative of a kind, 'and you…'."),
((135,"fluctus."),
"but better it is the stirred-up to settle waves.",
"But it is better first to settle the waves you have stirred.",
"'praestat' — impersonal, 'it is better', with the infinitive 'componere'. 'componere' — to put together, settle, set in order: Neptune turns from punishment to restoring calm, the business at hand. 'motos… fluctus' frames the clause."),
((136,"luetis."),
"Later to me with no like penalty your offences you will pay for.",
"Another time you will pay me for your crimes with no such light penalty.",
"'Post' — afterwards, another time. 'mihi' — dative, 'to me', the one owed the penalty. 'non simili poena' — 'with no similar penalty', understatement (litotes): with a far heavier one than this rebuke. 'commissa' — 'things committed', offences, a neuter plural participle used as a noun. 'luo' — pay, atone for."),
((137,"vestro:"),
"Hasten your flight, and to your king these words say:",
"Be off at once, and give your king this message:",
"'Maturate fugam' — 'speed up your flight', a brusque dismissal. 'regi vestro' — 'your king' is Aeolus, and the 'your' is contemptuous: king of you, and of nothing more. The colon opens the message itself."),
((139,"datum."),
"not to him the rule of the sea and the fierce trident,\nbut to me by lot was given.",
"not to him was dominion over the sea and the dread trident allotted, but to me.",
"'illi… mihi' — 'to him… to me', datives set against each other at the heads of their clauses. 'sorte' — 'by lot': after the fall of Saturn his three sons drew lots for sky, sea and underworld, as Poseidon recalls in Iliad 15. 'datum (est)' agrees with the nearer noun, or with the pair taken as one thing. 'tridentem' — the trident, the three-pronged spear that is the sea-god's sceptre."),
((140,"domos;"),
"He holds huge rocks,\nyour homes, Eurus;",
"His domain is the monstrous crags — your homes, East Wind;",
"'ille' — 'that one', Aeolus, said with contempt. 'vestras… domos' stands in apposition to 'saxa': the rocks are your houses. Neptune names Eurus ('Eure', vocative) but means all the winds — 'vestras' is plural. The rocks are the cave-prison of lines 52–54."),
((141,"regnet.”"),
"in that hall let him swagger,\nAeolus, and, the prison of the winds shut, let him reign.”",
"let Aeolus strut in that hall of his, and be king there — with the winds' prison shut.”",
"'iactet' and 'regnet' are jussive subjunctives, 'let him…'. 'se iactare' — to toss oneself about, to show off. 'illa… aula' — 'that court of his', contemptuous again. 'clauso… carcere' — ablative absolute, 'the prison having been shut': Aeolus may reign only while the doors stay closed, a pointed reply to his letting the winds out at lines 81–83. The speech ends here, with Greenough's closing mark."),
((143,"reducit."),
"So he speaks, and quicker than the word the swollen seas he calms,\nand the gathered clouds puts to flight, and the sun brings back.",
"So he speaks, and faster than his speaking he stills the swollen seas, routs the massed clouds and brings the sun back.",
"'Sic ait' — 'so he speaks', the formula that closes a speech, as 'talia fatur' opened it. 'dicto citius' — 'quicker than the word', ablative of comparison: the calm comes before the speech is done. The three verbs, 'placat… fugat… reducit', each closing its clause, make an orderly sequence where the storm was chaos. 'tumida aequora' is elided, 'tumid' aequora'."),
((145,"scopulo;"),
"Cymothoe together with Triton, straining, from the sharp\nrock push off the ships;",
"Cymothoe and Triton, heaving together, shove the ships off the jagged rock;",
"Cymothoe is a Nereid, one of the sea-nymphs, daughters of Nereus; Triton is Neptune's son, a merman, the sea's trumpeter. 'adnixus' (from 'adnitor', Greenough's unassimilated spelling of 'annixus') — 'leaning hard against', putting his shoulder to it. 'acuto… scopulo' — ablative of separation, 'from the sharp rock', split across the line-end. 'navis' is the -is accusative plural. The plural verb follows the two subjects joined by 'simul et'."),
((145,"tridenti;"),
"he himself lifts with his trident;",
"the god himself prises them up with his trident;",
"'ipse' — Neptune himself, as against his helpers. 'tridenti' is ablative, with the i-stem ending -i: 'with the trident'. The god levers the stranded ships free."),
((147,"undas."),
"and he opens the vast sandbanks, and tempers the sea,\nand on light wheels he glides over the tops of the waves.",
"He clears a way through the vast sandbanks, soothes the sea, and skims on light wheels over the surface of the waves.",
"'syrtis' here is a common noun, any sandbank (compare the Syrtes of line 111), in the -is accusative plural. 'aperit' — clears a passage through them. 'temperat' is the word used of Aeolus calming the winds (line 57). 'rotis… levibus' wraps round 'summas', and 'summas… undas' round the verb. The sea-god driving his chariot over the waves comes from Iliad 13, where Poseidon's horses carry him across the sea and the sea-creatures come up to greet their lord."),
((150,"ministrat;"),
"And just as in a great people, when, as often, there has arisen\na riot, and rages in its spirits the base rabble,\nand now torches and stones fly — fury supplies the weapons;",
"And as so often happens in a great nation, when a riot has broken out and the lowborn mob runs wild with passion, and already torches and stones are flying — frenzy finds its own weapons —",
"The poem's first simile, and a startling one. Homer compares crowds to the sea (Iliad 2, the assembly heaving like waves); Virgil turns it round and compares the sea to a crowd, explaining nature by Roman politics. 'Ac veluti… cum' — 'and just as when…', answered by 'sic' in line 154. 'coorta est' — 'has arisen', a thing that happens time and again ('saepe'). 'volgus' is Greenough's spelling of 'vulgus', the older vowel, as in 'vortex' (117). 'animis' — 'in their spirits', ablative of respect. 'furor arma ministrat' — 'rage hands out the weapons', whatever lies to hand: a much-quoted half-line. A Roman reader would think of the street riots of the late Republic."),
((152,"adstant;"),
"then, weighty in devotion and services, if by chance some man\nthey have caught sight of, they fall silent, and with ears pricked they stand by;",
"then, if they chance to catch sight of some man whose devotion and services give him weight, they fall silent and stand still with ears alert;",
"'pietate gravem ac meritis' — 'weighty with devotion and services': 'gravis' is the Roman virtue of 'gravitas', moral weight and authority; 'pietas' is Aeneas's own virtue (line 10). 'si… quem' — after 'si', 'quis/qui' means 'some, any' ('quem' = 'aliquem'); you'll see this again. 'conspexere' = 'conspexerunt'. 'arrectis auribus' — ablative absolute, 'with ears pricked up', a picture from animals listening. 'adstant' — Greenough's unassimilated spelling of 'astant'. The man is left unnamed; readers have tried to name him, but Virgil keeps him a type."),
((156,"secundo."),
"he rules with words their spirits, and their hearts soothes —\nso all the crashing of the sea fell, once, over the waters\nlooking out, the father, and borne along under open sky,\nturns his horses, and flying gives rein to his willing chariot.",
"and he governs their passions with his words and calms their hearts — so all the uproar of the sea died away, once the father, gazing out over the waters and riding under a sky now clear, wheeled his horses and, flying, gave free rein to his easy-running chariot.",
"'ille' — the man of weight; words ('dictis') are his only weapon against the mob's stones. 'mulcet' — strokes, soothes: Aeneas will do the same for his men at line 197. 'sic' answers 'veluti' (148) and brings us back to the sea. 'cecidit fragor' — 'the crashing fell', died down. 'aequora postquam' — 'postquam' placed after a word of its own clause, common in verse. 'genitor' — 'the father', a title of honour for a great god, here Neptune. 'caelo aperto' — under a sky now opened, cleared. 'curru… secundo' — the chariot 'following', running easily behind the horses; 'dat lora' — 'gives the reins', lets the horses run."),
((158,"oras."),
"Worn out, the men of Aeneas, the shores which are nearest, by sailing\nstrive to reach, and toward Libya's coasts are turned.",
"Aeneas's weary men strain to reach whatever shore lies nearest, and turn toward the coasts of Libya.",
"'Aeneadae' — 'sons of Aeneas', a patronymic used of his followers; Lucretius' poem opens 'Aeneadum genetrix', mother of the Aeneadae, meaning the Romans, who saw themselves as Aeneas's people. 'quae proxima litora' — 'the shores which (are) nearest': the antecedent is drawn into the relative clause, so 'they strive to reach the nearest shores'. 'cursu' — 'in their course', by sailing. 'vertuntur' — middle in sense, 'they turn themselves'. Libya is the Roman name for North Africa in general; they will land not far from Carthage."),
((159,"locus:"),
"There is in a long recess a place:",
"There is a place, deep within a long inlet:",
"'Est… locus' — 'there is a place', the standard opening of a set description (an ecphrasis): the story pauses to paint a scene, and you will meet 'est locus' again. 'secessu longo' — a long withdrawal of the coast, a deep inlet. The harbour is modelled on two in the Odyssey: the harbour by the Cyclopes' land in Book 9 and the harbour of Phorcys on Ithaca, with its cave of the nymphs, in Book 13. Servius, the ancient commentator, already called it an imagined place."),
((161,"reductos."),
"an island a harbour\nmakes by the barrier of its sides, on which every wave from the deep\nis broken, and into withdrawn bays splits itself.",
"an island forms a harbour with the screen of its flanks, on which every swell from the open sea breaks and parts into the curving bays within.",
"'obiectu laterum' — 'by the throwing-across of its sides': the island lies across the mouth of the inlet, and its flanks shelter the water. 'quibus' — 'on which' (the sides). 'omnis… unda' — 'every wave', subject and adjective far apart, the wave travelling the length of the line. 'inque sinus… reductos' — 'and into withdrawn bays', 'reductus' meaning set back. 'scindit sese' — splits itself. 'omnis' here is nominative singular."),
((164,"silent;"),
"On this side and that vast cliffs and twin threaten\ntoward the sky crags, beneath whose summit far and wide\nthe waters, safe, are silent;",
"On either hand vast cliffs and twin crags loom up against the sky, and beneath their peaks the sheltered water lies hushed far and wide;",
"'Hinc atque hinc' — 'from here and from here', on both sides. 'minantur in caelum' — 'threaten into the sky', rise steep and menacing. 'gemini… scopuli' — 'twin crags', split across the line-end. 'quorum sub vertice' — 'beneath whose peak'. 'aequora tuta silent' — 'the safe waters are silent', stillness after the storm's noise; 'silent' is the word of the hushed crowd in the simile (152)."),
((165,"umbra."),
"then with quivering woods a backdrop\nfrom above, and a black grove overhangs with bristling shade.",
"above them rises a backdrop of shimmering woods, and a dark grove looms overhead in its shaggy shade.",
"'scaena' (Greenough's spelling; also 'scena') is a theatre word, the stage's painted back wall: the woods rise behind the harbour like a stage-set, and the reader sits in the audience. 'coruscis' — quivering, flickering with light. 'horrenti… umbra' — 'with bristling shade', the trees standing up like hair on end ('horreo'). 'atrum nemus' — a black grove; a 'nemus' is often a sacred wood. The verb of the first clause ('est') is understood. 'horrentique atrum' is elided, 'horrentiqu' atrum'."),
((168,"domus:"),
"Under the facing front, of hanging rocks, a cave;\nwithin, fresh waters, and of living stone seats,\nthe home of the nymphs:",
"Under the cliff-face opposite is a cave of overhanging rock, with fresh water inside and seats of natural stone — a dwelling of the nymphs;",
"No verb at all: a list, as in an inventory. 'Fronte sub adversa' — 'under the facing front', the cliff that fronts the harbour mouth. 'scopulis pendentibus' — 'of hanging rocks', ablative of description. 'aquae dulces' — 'sweet waters', fresh water, a sailor's first need. 'vivo… saxo' — 'living rock', stone left as it grew, not quarried. The cave of the nymphs comes from the harbour of Phorcys in Odyssey 13, where Odysseus is set ashore asleep on Ithaca."),
((169,"morsu."),
"here weary ships no cables\nat all hold, no anchor ties them with its hooked bite.",
"here no mooring-ropes are needed to hold the weary ships, no anchor to grip them with its hooked tooth.",
"'fessas… navis' — the ships themselves are weary, like their crews; 'navis' is the -is accusative plural. 'non… ulla' — 'not any', emphatic. 'unco… morsu' — 'with its hooked bite', the anchor's fluke biting the sea-floor. Homer says the same of the harbour in Odyssey 9: no need there of cables or anchor-stones."),
((171,"subit;"),
"Here, with seven ships gathered from the whole\nnumber, Aeneas enters;",
"Into this haven Aeneas comes with seven ships gathered from all his fleet;",
"'Huc' — 'to here', motion towards. 'septem… collectis navibus' — ablative absolute, 'seven ships having been gathered', which English says as 'with seven ships'. 'omni ex numero' — 'out of the whole number': he sailed with twenty, as he tells his mother at line 381, so thirteen are missing; they will come back into the story later in the book. 'subit' — goes in, enters the harbour."),
((173,"ponunt."),
"and with a great love of the land,\ndisembarking, the longed-for the Trojans take possession of, the sand,\nand their limbs, soaked with brine, on the shore they lay down.",
"and the Trojans, aching for solid ground, climb out and take possession of the sand they had prayed for, and stretch their brine-soaked bodies on the beach.",
"'telluris amore' — 'with love of the land', an objective genitive: they long for the land. 'egressi' — 'having gone out', disembarked. 'potiuntur' takes the ablative: 'they make themselves masters of the longed-for sand'. 'Troes' is the Greek nominative plural of 'Tros', a Trojan. 'tabentis' — 'dripping, wasting', the -is accusative plural agreeing with 'artus'. 'sale' — 'with salt', the sea."),
((176,"flammam."),
"And first from flint a spark struck out Achates,\nand caught the fire in leaves, and dry around it\nfuel gave, and snatched up in the tinder the flame.",
"First Achates strikes a spark from flint, catches the fire in leaves, packs dry kindling all round it, and quickly coaxes a flame from the tinder.",
"'silici' — 'from the flint', a dative with a verb of taking away. 'excudit' — present tense of narrative; 'succepit', 'dedit', 'rapuit' switch to the perfect, a quick series of acts. 'succepit' is Greenough's spelling of 'suscepit', 'caught it (from beneath)'. 'nutrimenta' — food for the fire. 'rapuit in fomite flammam' — 'seized the flame in the tinder', made it catch at once. Fire-making, the first act ashore, is told in close detail, a Homeric attention to ordinary work; compare the end of Odyssey 5, where shipwrecked Odysseus buries himself in leaves like an ember banked to keep a fire alive."),
((179,"saxo."),
"Then Ceres spoiled by the waves and Ceres' weapons\nthey bring out, weary of their fortunes, and the grain recovered\nboth to parch they prepare with flames and to crush with stone.",
"Then, worn out by their troubles, they bring out the sea-soaked grain and the tools for making bread, and set about parching the salvaged corn over the fire and grinding it with stone.",
"'Cererem' — Ceres, goddess of grain, for the grain itself (metonymy, a god's name for what the god governs, as 'Bacchus' for wine). 'Cerealia arma' — 'the weapons of Ceres', a mock-heroic name for the kitchen gear: mills, troughs, sieves. 'Cerealiaque arma' is elided, 'Cerealiaqu' arma'. 'fessi rerum' — 'weary of things', of their troubles; a genitive with an adjective. 'fruges receptas' — the grain saved from the wreck. 'et… et' — 'both… and': grain was parched before it was ground."),
((183,"Caici."),
"Aeneas a crag meanwhile climbs, and the whole\nview far and wide over the sea seeks, if any Antheus\ntossed by the wind he might see, and the Phrygian biremes,\nor Capys, or on high sterns the arms of Caicus.",
"Meanwhile Aeneas climbs a crag and searches the whole wide prospect of the sea, in hope of seeing Antheus, storm-tossed, somewhere, and the Phrygian galleys, or Capys, or the arms of Caicus on a high stern.",
"'interea' — meanwhile, while the men cook. 'omnem prospectum… petit' — 'seeks the whole view', scans the whole sea. 'Anthea si quem… videat' — 'if he might see any Antheus', Antheus or anyone at all; 'si quem' again = 'si aliquem', and the subjunctive 'videat' gives the hope behind his looking. 'Anthea' (Antheus) and 'Capyn' (Capys) are Greek accusatives; 'Caici' is genitive. 'Phrygias' — Trojan (Phrygia was the region round Troy). 'biremis' — biremes, ships with two banks of oars, in the -is accusative plural. 'arma… in puppibus' — shields hung on the high sterns, marking a captain's ship. The look-out from a height followed by a stag-hunt comes from Odysseus on Circe's island, Odyssey 10."),
((185,"errantis;"),
"A ship in sight, none; three on the shore stags\nhe spies wandering;",
"No ship is in sight; but he spies three stags roaming the shore;",
"'Navem… nullam' — 'no ship', the adjective held back for the bad news. No 'but' joins the two clauses (asyndeton): disappointment and the new sight stand side by side. 'tris… errantis' — -is accusative plurals again, agreeing with 'cervos'. 'litore' — ablative of place, 'on the shore'."),
((186,"agmen."),
"these whole herds follow\nfrom behind, and through the valleys a long column grazes.",
"whole herds follow behind them, and the long column grazes its way along the valleys.",
"'armenta' — properly herds of cattle, here of deer. 'a tergo' — 'from the back', behind. 'agmen' is a military word, an army column on the march: the deer are described as an army, with leaders and rank and file to come. 'vallis' — -is accusative plural after 'per'."),
((188,"Achates;"),
"He halted here, and the bow in hand and the swift arrows\nsnatched up, the weapons faithful Achates was carrying;",
"He stopped there and snatched up the bow and swift arrows that loyal Achates carried for him;",
"'Constitit hic' — 'he stood still here', the hunter's pause. 'celeris' — -is accusative plural with 'sagittas'. 'quae tela' — 'which weapons': the antecedent (bow and arrows) restated inside the relative clause as 'tela'. 'fidus Achates' — 'faithful Achates', his standing epithet; the pair becomes a fixed formula through the poem, as Homer's heroes have theirs. Achates is Aeneas's armour-bearer and constant companion."),
((191,"turbam;"),
"and the leaders themselves first, their heads high bearing\nwith tree-like antlers, he lays low, then the commons, and the whole\nhe throws into confusion, driving with his shafts among the leafy woods, crowd;",
"the leaders themselves he lays low first, carrying their heads high with branching antlers, then the common herd, and with his arrows he drives the whole crowd in disorder through the leafy woods;",
"The hunt is told in the language of war and politics: 'ductores' (generals), 'volgus' (the common people, the word of the riot simile, line 149), 'turbam' (the mob). 'ferentis' — -is accusative plural, agreeing with 'ductores'. 'cornibus arboreis' — 'tree-like horns', branching antlers. 'sternit' — 'strews', lays low. 'nemora inter frondea' — the preposition after its first word (anastrophe), common in verse. 'miscet agens' — 'drives and throws into confusion'."),
((193,"aequet."),
"nor sooner does he stop than seven huge, as victor,\nbodies he lays on the ground, and the number with the ships makes equal.",
"nor does he stop until, triumphant, he has stretched seven huge carcasses on the ground, one for every ship.",
"'prius… quam' — 'before, until', split in two (tmesis of 'priusquam'); with the subjunctive ('fundat', 'aequet') it looks ahead to an aim. 'humi' — locative, 'on the ground'. 'victor' — 'as victor', a battlefield word again. 'numerum cum navibus aequet' — 'makes the number equal with the ships': a deer for each of the seven crews. In Odyssey 10 Odysseus kills one great stag for his men; Virgil's hero kills seven."),
((194,"omnes."),
"From there the harbour he seeks, and among his comrades shares them out, all.",
"Then he makes for the harbour and divides the meat among all his comrades.",
"'Hinc' — from here (and so, next). 'partitur' — deponent, 'divides, shares out'; the object, the deer, is understood. 'socios… in omnes' — 'among all his comrades', with 'omnes' at the line-end: no one is left out. Sharing the spoils fairly is a leader's duty."),
((197,"mulcet:"),
"Next, the wine which kindly Acestes had stowed in jars\non the Trinacrian shore and, hero that he was, given them as they left,\nhe shares out, and with words their grieving hearts he soothes:",
"Next he shares out the wine that good Acestes, like the prince he was, had loaded into jars on the Sicilian shore and given them as they sailed, and he soothes their grieving hearts with these words:",
"'deinde' belongs with 'dividit' — 'next he shares out' — though it stands inside the relative clause; the antecedent 'vina' comes first, the relative 'quae' after it. 'bonus… Acestes' — 'kind Acestes', the adjective a line's length from its noun. Acestes is a Sicilian king of Trojan descent, who had given them hospitality on the island; Book 5 returns to him. 'onerarat' = 'oneraverat', a shortened (syncopated) pluperfect, 'had loaded'; 'cadis' — 'in jars'. 'Trinacrio' — Sicilian: Trinacria, 'the three-cornered', is a Greek name for Sicily. 'heros' — 'the hero', a title of honour for Acestes. 'pectora mulcet' repeats the words of the statesman in the simile (153): Aeneas calms his men as Neptune calmed the sea. The colon opens his speech, which begins the next part."),
]
TITLE = "Neptunus. Portus Libycus"
TITLE_EN = "Neptune Stills the Storm — Aeneid I.102–197"
PART = "Neptune Stills the Storm (I.102–197)"
ABOUT = ("The storm Juno ordered breaks over the Trojan fleet: oars snap, ships are flung on reefs and sandbanks, one goes down with all hands before Aeneas's eyes. "
         "Then Neptune, lord of the sea, feels his realm in uproar, rebukes the winds for trespassing on it, and calms the waves — and Virgil gives his first simile, comparing the god to a statesman who quiets a rioting crowd with a word. "
         "Seven ships limp into a sheltered harbour on the Libyan coast; Achates lights a fire, Aeneas climbs a crag to look for the missing ships, finds none, and shoots seven stags, one for each crew. "
         "As in the first part, Virgil is rewriting the Odyssey: the storm, the harbour with its cave of the nymphs and the hunt on a strange shore are all Homer's, retold for Rome.")
